---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  const posts = {
    "shanghai-street-food-2026": {
      title: "Shanghai Street Food: 12 Must-Try Stalls in 2026",
      date: "2026-07-15",
      excerpt: "From shengjianbao in the French Concession to scallion pancakes near the Bund, these are the street food stalls that define Shanghai.",
      sections: [
        { type: "p", text: "Shanghai's street food scene is legendary, and 2026 is no exception. From dawn until late night, the city hums with the sizzle of woks, the hiss of bamboo steamers, and the intoxicating aroma of sesame oil, soy, and caramelized sugar that spills from alleyway stalls into the surrounding streets. What follows is not a curated list for tourists — it is a local's route through the city's surviving street food heartland, built from decades of morning commutes, late-night cravings, and the stubborn traditions that refuse to be sanitized into food court versions of themselves." },
        { type: "h2", text: "Shengjianbao: The Undisputed King" },
        { type: "p", text: "No street food defines Shanghai more decisively than shengjianbao — pan-fried pork buns with a golden, lacy crust that shatters at first bite. Yang's Fried Dumpling on Huanghe Road remains the benchmark, with a queue that snakes around the block from 8 am until they sell out, usually around 2 pm. Each bun is a study in textural contrast: the base is fried to a deep bronze in a giant cast-iron pan filmed with oil and water, creating a crisp platform that supports a soft, yeasted crown. The filling — seasoned pork with ginger and scallion suspended in a jellied broth — erupts in a torrent when bitten. Order four per person, add black vinegar and a smear of chili oil, and eat standing at the narrow counter under the watchful gaze of dumpling chefs who fold each bun by hand at a rate of one every four seconds. For a smaller, family-run alternative, head to Shen Xiao Mei on Shaanxi Road, where the buns are slightly sweeter and the bottoms achieve a caramelized depth that Yang's, for all its glory, does not quite match." },
        { type: "h3", text: "The Shengjianbao Ritual" },
        { type: "p", text: "Eating shengjianbao correctly requires technique: grip the bun with chopsticks, bite a small hole in the side to release the scalding steam, sip the broth (carefully — it is volcanic), then dip the exposed interior into vinegar and finish in two more bites. Watch locals do it — the sequence is practiced and instinctive, the same motion repeated thousands of times." },
        { type: "h2", text: "Scallion Pancakes: The Hidden Art" },
        { type: "p", text: "Cong you bing — scallion pancakes — appear deceptively simple but demand extraordinary technique. The best version in Shanghai comes from a nameless cart outside the No. 1 Food Department Store on Nanjing Road, where a woman in her seventies has been making them for forty-two years. She coils each pancake by hand, stretching the dough into a paper-thin sheet, layering it with minced scallions, salt, and a whisper of Sichuan peppercorn, then coiling it into a tight spiral that she flattens and griddles until the exterior is shatteringly crisp and the interior remains chewy and laminated. The resulting pancake is a study in geometry — concentric rings of dough that pull apart like an accordion, each layer carrying the fragrant punch of scallion. She charges RMB 6 per pancake and sells about three hundred a day. There is no sign, no menu, no social media presence. You find it by following the scent of melting dough and the crunch of customers peeling layers apart." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Where the Locals Queue" },
        { type: "p", text: "Beyond the famous names, Shanghai's street food soul lives in its longtang — the surviving lane-house neighborhoods where vendors operate from converted ground-floor rooms. Near the City God Temple, a second-floor window dispenses the city's best ci fan: warm glutinous rice wrapped around a core of youtiao (fried dough stick), pork floss, and pickled radish, squeezed into a compact torpedo that fits in one hand. In the old French Concession, a skewer stall on Wukang Road serves lamb and cumin skewers that draw crowds from 7 pm until midnight, the smoke from their charcoal grill drifting up past the plane trees." },
        { type: "h2", text: "Douhua: The Sweet Finish" },
        { type: "p", text: "Shanghai street food does not do dessert in the Western sense, but it has douhua — silken tofu served in a ginger-scented syrup that is neither hot nor cold but somewhere in the warm middle, the temperature of comfort. A vendor on Yunnan Road has been serving it from a tin pot since 1985, ladling the pale, trembling curds into bowls and topping them with osmanthus honey, red beans, and a single candied date. It is subtle, soothing, and the perfect palate cleanser after a tour of the city's more aggressive flavors. Most servings cost between RMB 8 and 12." },
        { type: "h2", text: "Navigating the Scene in 2026" },
        { type: "p", text: "A few practical notes for the street food explorer: bring cash, as many stalls still do not take digital payments. Go early — the best shengjianbao are gone by 1 pm, and the best scallion pancakes by 11 am. Do not be deterred by queues; a long line in Shanghai is the most reliable quality signal. And finally, trust your nose before you trust any app — the smell of sesame oil and caramelizing sugar will guide you to better food than any algorithmic recommendation." },
      ]
    },
    "hairy-crab-season-guide": {
      title: "The Ultimate Guide to Hairy Crab Season in Shanghai",
      date: "2026-07-02",
      excerpt: "Everything you need to know about Shanghai's most anticipated food event: when to go, where to eat, and how to order.",
      sections: [
        { type: "p", text: "Hairy crab season — da zha xie ji jie in Chinese — is the most anticipated culinary event on the Shanghai calendar. From late September through December, the entire city enters a state of collective obsession with a single ingredient: a palm-sized freshwater crab named for the dark, velvety hair that covers its claws. The season is brief, expensive, and culturally significant enough to shape the operating schedules of Shanghai's finest restaurants. This guide covers everything you need: when to eat, where to book, how to choose your crab, and how to eat it with the grace that the occasion demands." },
        { type: "h2", text: "When the Season Actually Starts" },
        { type: "p", text: "The official start of hairy crab season is loosely pegged to the Mid-Autumn Festival, which usually falls in late September or early October. But knowledgeable eaters know to wait until mid-October. Early-season crabs are still molting and have not yet developed the full roe and fat that make the experience transcendent. The golden window runs from the second week of October through the end of November. Female crabs peak first (October to early November) with their bright orange roe; male crabs peak later (November to early December) with a creamy white organ called 'gao' that connoisseurs prize even above roe. By mid-December, the quality begins to decline — the roe firms up, the meat becomes less sweet, and prices drop accordingly. For the finest experience, book your crab feast for the last two weeks of October." },
        { type: "h3", text: "The Science of the Season" },
        { type: "p", text: "The crabs migrate from Yangcheng Lake — the most prestigious source — where the mineral-rich lake bed and controlled farming conditions produce the sweetest, most tender specimens. As water temperatures drop in autumn, the crabs' metabolism slows and they store more fat and glycogen, which translates directly into the prized richness of the roe and the sweetness of the meat. This is not folklore; it is biology, and it is why the timing of your reservation matters as much as the restaurant you choose." },
        { type: "h2", text: "Where to Eat Hairy Crab in Shanghai" },
        { type: "p", text: "The spectrum of hairy crab dining runs from dedicated crab houses to Michelin-starred tasting menus. For the classic experience, Wang Bao He on Fuzhou Road is a century-old establishment that serves nothing but crab in season — male and female steamed whole, served with a dipping sauce of black vinegar, ginger, and brown sugar. The second-floor dining room, with its wooden partitions and faded grandeur, has hosted crab feasts since the 1920s. For high-end interpretation, Xin Rong Ji offers a multi-course hairy crab menu that builds from a delicate crab roe and tofu custard to whole steamed crabs and finishes with crab roe tossed with hand-pulled noodles. The tasting menu costs approximately RMB 1,200 per person and books out weeks in advance. In the middle range, YongFoo Elite's garden setting provides an elegant backdrop for a crab-focused dinner, with the added charm of their crab roe xiaolongbao — a dumpling that manages to contain a second, smaller universe of flavor within its already perfect form." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Choosing Male vs Female" },
        { type: "p", text: "This is the most consequential decision you will make at a hairy crab meal. Female crabs (ci xie) carry the bright orange roe — tightly packed, grainy, and intensely savory. Male crabs (gong xie) carry 'gao' — a white, custard-like substance that is creamier and more subtle. The general rule: order female in October and November, male in November and December. If you are dining in the peak window and can afford it, order both. Most restaurants will let you specify the ratio." },
        { type: "h2", text: "The Etiquette of Eating Hairy Crab" },
        { type: "p", text: "Hairy crab is not fast food. It is a meditative, hands-on process that rewards patience. The crab arrives whole, steamed with ginger slices and tied with twine. You will be given a pair of scissors, a small pick, and a tiny fork — the 'crab tools' that transform the meal into a surgical operation. The correct sequence: remove the legs first and set them aside, pry open the belly shell, remove the inedible gills and stomach sac (bitter, do not eat), then scoop out the roe or gao with the tiny fork, dip it in the vinegar-ginger sauce, and eat it plain. Then crack the legs with the scissors, extract the meat with the pick, and repeat. A single crab takes 15 to 25 minutes to eat properly. Conversations over hairy crab are slower, quieter, more deliberate. This is the point." },
        { type: "h3", text: "What to Drink" },
        { type: "p", text: "Hairy crab demands a specific pairing: warm Shaoxing huangjiu (yellow wine), preferably a 10-15 year vintage, served in a small ceramic flask heated in hot water. The wine's nutty, oxidized notes cut through the richness of the roe and complement the sweetness of the meat. Some restaurants also serve it with ginger-infused tea. Avoid red wine, which clashes with the crab's iodine notes. A crisp Chenin Blanc or a dry Riesling from Alsace can work in a pinch, but the purist's choice is always huangjiu." },
      ]
    },
    "michelin-shanghai-2026": {
      title: "Michelin Guide Shanghai 2026: All the Stars",
      date: "2026-06-18",
      excerpt: "A complete rundown of every Michelin-starred restaurant in Shanghai, from three-star temples to new one-star entries.",
      sections: [
        { type: "p", text: "The Michelin Guide Shanghai 2026, unveiled on June 15 at a ceremony at the W Hotel on the Bund, continues to cement the city's status as one of the world's great dining destinations. This year's edition features 54 starred restaurants, up from 50 in 2025, with notable movements across every tier. The guide's ongoing expansion reflects Shanghai's deepening culinary maturity — not just in the high-end Western and Japanese segments that have traditionally dominated the guide, but in the growing recognition of regional Chinese cuisines that have long been overlooked by the red guidebook." },
        { type: "h2", text: "Three-Star: The Unshakeable Pinnacle" },
        { type: "p", text: "Shanghai retains its two three-star establishments in 2026. Ultraviolet by Paul Pairet holds its three stars for the seventh consecutive year — a sensory dining experience that remains without parallel anywhere in the world, combining precisely choreographed multi-sensory projections with French-Asian cooking that challenges every preconception of what a restaurant meal can be. The second three-star, T'ang Court at the Langham Hotel, maintains its standing for Cantonese haute cuisine of extraordinary refinement, with a dim sum program that rivals the best in Hong Kong. Neither restaurant shows any sign of relinquishing its third star, and the gap between three-star and two-star in Shanghai remains significant." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Pricing and Booking Reality" },
        { type: "p", text: "Ultraviolet serves one seating per night at RMB 6,000 per person and requires booking three months in advance. T'ang Court is more accessible at approximately RMB 1,500 per person for a tasting menu, but weekend reservations still require two to three weeks' lead time." },
        { type: "h2", text: "Two-Star: The Big Story of 2026" },
        { type: "p", text: "The marquee announcement of this year's guide is Xin Rong Ji's elevation from one star to two stars. This recognition of chef Zhang Yong's refined Taizhou cuisine — a regional style from Zhejiang Province that centers on seafood, fermented ingredients, and precise knife work — marks a significant milestone for the guide's engagement with authentic Chinese regional cooking. Xin Rong Ji joins an elite two-star cohort that includes Fu He Hui (vegetarian tasting menus that have redefined plant-based fine dining), Da Vittorio (Italian elegance with a Shanghai accent), and Yi Long Court (Cantonese precision at the Peninsula Hotel). The two-star tier now represents Shanghai's competitive sweet spot: restaurants with world-class technique and distinct creative voices that have not yet crossed into the extreme pricing and access barriers of the three-star level." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Regional Cuisine Rising" },
        { type: "p", text: "Xin Rong Ji's promotion is part of a broader trend. This year's guide features more representation from Chinese regional cuisines than any previous edition — including Chuan (Sichuan), Lu (Shandong), and Hui (Anhui) restaurants — signaling that Michelin's famously conservative Chinese palate is finally broadening. For diners, this means the starred list is more useful than ever as a guide to China's extraordinary culinary diversity." },
        { type: "h2", text: "New One-Star Entries Worth Noting" },
        { type: "p", text: "Four restaurants received their first star this year. The most talked-about is Ling Long, a 14-seat counter on the Bund where chef Jason Liu presents a tasting menu that maps the culinary geography of China through modern techniques — think 'Sichuan in three bites' or 'A journey along the Yangtze.' The second notable debut is Xing Ye, a Cantonese barbecue specialist that elevates siu mei (roast meats) to a level rarely seen outside Hong Kong, with its honey-glazed char siu achieving a caramelization that critics have described as 'disturbingly perfect.' The other two new entrants — a French-Japanese fusion concept and a refined dumpling house — round out the list but have generated less excitement." },
        { type: "h3", text: "The Bib Gourmand List" },
        { type: "p", text: "Michelin's more affordable Bib Gourmand category grew to 38 restaurants in 2026, reflecting the guide's acknowledgment that Shanghai's real culinary energy is in its mid-range. New additions include a Lao Shanghai (old Shanghai) canteen serving hongshao rou that rivals Old Jesse's at half the price, and a Yunnan noodle shop in the French Concession where the cross-bridge rice noodles draw lunch queues that regularly exceed forty minutes." },
      ]
    },
    "shanghai-vs-hong-kong-dim-sum": {
      title: "Shanghai vs Hong Kong: The Great Dim Sum Debate",
      date: "2026-06-01",
      excerpt: "Two great food cities, two dim sum traditions. We compare the xiaolongbao, shao mai, and wontons from both cities side by side.",
      sections: [
        { type: "p", text: "The dim sum rivalry between Shanghai and Hong Kong is one of the great unresolved debates in Chinese gastronomy. Both cities unquestionably possess extraordinary dim sum cultures. But they start from fundamentally different places: Hong Kong's dim sum is the Cantonese tradition of yum cha — tea-drinking with small, steam-basket plates served in a chaotic, trolley-filled dining room. Shanghai's is a lineage of specific, artisan-focused snacks that evolved through temple fairs, street stalls, and lane-house kitchens into the refined forms we know today. The two cities do not actually compete in the same category, but the comparison is irresistible. Here is a side-by-side breakdown of how their signature dim sum items stack up." },
        { type: "h2", text: "Xiaolongbao: Shanghai's Claim to Supremacy" },
        { type: "p", text: "This is where Shanghai holds an unassailable advantage. Xiaolongbao is a Shanghai invention — specifically, a Nanxiang invention — and no amount of Hong Kong technique can erase that origin. The Shanghai version is characterized by a gossamer-thin wrapper (ideally translucent enough to see the broth sloshing inside), a pork filling seasoned with ginger and Shaoxing wine, and a generous amount of jellied stock that melts into soup during steaming. Hong Kong's xiaolongbao, introduced relatively recently via Taiwanese chains like Din Tai Fung, tend to have thicker, more resilient wrappers and fillings that incorporate more sugar and white pepper. The difference is not one of quality but of philosophy: Shanghai xiaolongbao are delicate, almost fragile, demanding careful handling; Hong Kong versions are engineered for reliability, designed to survive the high-volume turnover of a dim sum service. For the purist, the Shanghai version wins. But for consistency across a large meal, the Hong Kong approach has its logic." },
        { type: "h3", text: "The Vinegar Question" },
        { type: "p", text: "Shanghai serves xiaolongbao with black Zhenjiang vinegar and shredded ginger — the acidity cuts the richness of the broth. Hong Kong restaurants typically provide red vinegar or a blend, and often skip the ginger. This small difference signals the broader divide: Shanghai's approach is ingredient-driven and specific; Hong Kong's is about harmony across a larger spread of dishes." },
        { type: "h2", text: "Shao Mai: Hong Kong Takes the Lead" },
        { type: "p", text: "This one is not close. Hong Kong's shao mai — an open-topped dumpling filled with pork, shrimp, and shiitake mushrooms, crowned with a single orange crab roe dot — is a perfected form. The filling is bouncy and succulent, achieved through vigorous hand-chopping of the pork (never minced) that preserves texture. Shanghai's version uses sticky glutinous rice as the primary filling, studded with pork bits and mushrooms, wrapped in a yellow dough that is thicker and chewier. The rice version is satisfying in its own right — it is essentially a portable snack rather than a dim sum centerpiece — but it cannot compete with the sophistication and luxury of the Hong Kong version. Hong Kong wins this round definitively." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Har Gow: The Cantonese Masterpiece" },
        { type: "p", text: "Har gow — translucent shrimp dumplings — are a Cantonese invention and Hong Kong executes them with an artistry that Shanghai does not attempt to match. The pleating (at least 10 folds, ideally 13), the translucent starch wrapper that reveals the pink shrimp within, the filling's just-cooked snap — this is technique that requires years to master. Shanghai has no equivalent dish, and it is the single strongest argument for Hong Kong's dim sum supremacy." },
        { type: "h2", text: "Wontons: Different Universes" },
        { type: "p", text: "Shanghai wontons (hunda) are large, thick-skinned, and typically served in a clear broth with dried shrimp, laver (seaweed), and a drizzle of lard — a warming, substantial bowl that functions as a meal. The filling is pork and chopped vegetables (Chinese chives in winter, celery in summer). Hong Kong wontons are smaller, thinner-skinned, and stuffed with whole shrimp and pork, served in a delicate shrimp-shell broth. The Shanghai wonton is about comfort and home cooking; the Hong Kong wonton is about refinement and the quality of the sea. Neither is objectively superior — they serve different purposes. But for the cold winter months, the Shanghai wonton's embrace is hard to beat." },
        { type: "h3", text: "The Verdict" },
        { type: "p", text: "No single winner exists, but here is an honest assessment: Hong Kong offers a broader, more refined dim sum tradition overall, with greater variety and a deeper bench of expertly executed classics. Shanghai wins the specific battles that matter most to its identity — xiaolongbao and the wonton as comfort food — but loses the wider war. The best strategy is to enjoy both cities for what they uniquely offer and never force a choice." },
      ]
    },
    "vegetarian-shanghai-guide": {
      title: "Vegetarian Shanghai: A Plant-Based Food Guide",
      date: "2026-05-14",
      excerpt: "Shanghai has a rich Buddhist vegetarian tradition. From temple restaurants to modern plant-based dining, here is where to eat.",
      sections: [
        { type: "p", text: "Shanghai's vegetarian scene is one of the city's best-kept secrets, not because it is hidden but because it is so thoroughly integrated into the city's food culture that it rarely announces itself as a separate category. Two distinct vegetarian traditions coexist in the city: the Buddhist temple tradition of su cai, which dates back centuries and uses meat analogs made from tofu, wheat gluten, and mushrooms to recreate classic dishes without animal products, and a newer wave of modern plant-based dining that draws from global influences and has earned international recognition, including Michelin stars. This guide covers both worlds and helps you navigate them with confidence." },
        { type: "h2", text: "The Temple Tradition: Gong De Lin" },
        { type: "p", text: "Gong De Lin (功德林) on Nanjing Road is the most famous Buddhist vegetarian restaurant in Shanghai, with a history that stretches back to 1922. The restaurant was founded by a group of Buddhist devotees and has operated continuously for over a century, surviving war, revolution, and the city's transformation into a global financial hub. The menu is a marvel of culinary illusion — dishes that look, smell, and taste like pork, chicken, and fish but contain no animal products whatsoever. The 'sweet and sour pork' is made from seitan (wheat gluten) that has been deep-fried to a chewy crispness and coated in a translucent orange sauce. The 'eel' is actually shredded king oyster mushrooms, painstakingly hand-torn to mimic the texture of eel meat, then stir-fried in a dark soy and sugar glaze. The 'duck' is layered bean curd skin, pressed and braised until it develops a convincing meaty density. The dining room has been updated in recent years but retains a serene atmosphere, with Buddhist calligraphy on the walls and a clientele that skews older and more traditional. Prices are remarkably reasonable — most dishes cost between RMB 30 and 70 — making Gong De Lin one of the best values in the city." },
        { type: "h3", text: "What to Order at Gong De Lin" },
        { type: "p", text: "Start with the smoked 'fish' made from tofu skin, which arrives with a convincing smoky aroma and a flaky texture. Follow with the 'sweet and sour pork' — the benchmark dish that determines whether a vegetarian kitchen has mastered its craft. Finish with the 'Yangzhou fried rice,' which uses diced vegetables and egg substitute to recreate the original's satisfying texture. Do not skip the dim sum: the vegetable xiaolongbao are filled with a mushroom and bamboo shoot mixture that produces a convincing broth substitute." },
        { type: "h2", text: "Modern Excellence: Fu He Hui" },
        { type: "p", text: "If Gong De Lin represents the past of Shanghai's vegetarian tradition, Fu He Hui represents its future — and its present at the highest level. In 2024, Fu He Hui became one of the first vegetarian restaurants in China to earn two Michelin stars, a recognition that stunned the culinary establishment and validated years of quiet, obsessive work by chef Tony Lu. The restaurant serves a single tasting menu (approximately RMB 800 per person) that changes entirely with the seasons. A recent spring menu included: chilled bamboo shoot with huangjiu gelee, Sichuan pepper-scented morels on a bed of pea purée, lotus root wrapped around black truffle and grilled over charcoal, and a 'broth of twenty vegetables' that clarified for 48 hours into a liquid so pure and concentrated that it tastes like the essence of an entire farmers' market compressed into a teacup. Every dish is vegetarian, but the word 'vegetarian' feels reductive — this is cooking that happens to exclude meat, not cooking that compensates for its absence." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Booking and Practical Notes" },
        { type: "p", text: "Fu He Hui books out approximately two to three weeks in advance. The restaurant seats only 22 guests per service and offers lunch and dinner seatings Tuesday through Sunday. The tasting menu runs 12 to 15 courses and takes about two and a half hours. Wine pairing is available but the tea pairing — curated by a dedicated tea master who sources single-origin Chinese teas from small producers — is the more compelling option and adds approximately RMB 300 to the bill." },
        { type: "h2", text: "Mid-Range and Casual Vegetarian" },
        { type: "p", text: "Between the temple restaurants and the Michelin-starred destination, Shanghai has a growing mid-range vegetarian scene that caters to everyday dining. Wu Guan Tang (无观堂) on Julu Road serves Buddhist-inspired set lunches for RMB 88 that include a soup, a main protein (tofu or mushroom-based), a vegetable side, and a small dessert — a remarkable deal for the quality. For something less formal, Vegetarian Lifestyle on Huashan Road is a cafeteria-style restaurant where you fill a tray with vegetable dishes, braised tofu, and rice, paying by weight. The clientele is a mix of office workers, elderly locals, and health-conscious young professionals. The braised eggplant in garlic sauce is the standout." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Vegan-Friendly Convenience" },
        { type: "p", text: "Shanghai is also one of the most vegan-friendly cities in Asia for convenience eating. Most family-run noodle shops will happily prepare a bowl of su mian (vegetarian noodles) with mushrooms, greens, and clear broth if you specify 'bu yao rou' (no meat). The city's many Muslim-owned noodle restaurants are also reliable choices, as their cuisine is naturally lamb-centric but includes excellent vegetable dishes and flatbreads." },
      ]
    },
  };
  return Object.entries(posts).map(function (entry) {
    var slug = entry[0];
    var post = entry[1];
    return { params: { slug: slug }, props: { post: post } };
  });
}

var post = Astro.props.post;
if (!post) return Astro.redirect("/404");

var slug = Astro.params.slug;
---

<Layout
  fullTitle={post.title} title={post.title}
  description={post.excerpt}
>
  <main>
    <section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-20 md:pt-28 pb-12">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <nav class="text-sm text-slate-400 mb-6">
          <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent-600 transition-colors">Home</a>
          <span class="mx-2">/</span>
          <a href="/blog" class="hover:text-accent-600 transition-colors">Blog</a>
        </nav>
        <time class="text-xs text-slate-400 font-medium">{post.date}</time>
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl tracking-tighter leading-none font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-2">{post.title}</h1>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8 pb-24">
      <article class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="text-lg text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">{post.excerpt}</p>
        <div class="mt-8 space-y-4 text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
          {post.sections.map(function (s) {
            if (s.type === "h2") return <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-8 mb-3">{s.text}</h2>;
            if (s.type === "h3") return <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-slate-800 mt-6 mb-2">{s.text}</h3>;
            return <p>{s.text}</p>;
          })}
        </div>
        <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-slate-100">
          <a href="/blog" class="text-sm font-medium text-accent-600 hover:text-accent-700">&larr; Back to Blog</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </section>
  </main>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BlogPosting",
    "headline": post.title,
    "description": post.excerpt,
    "datePublished": post.date,
    "dateModified": post.date,
    "url": "https://shanghai-restaurant.com/blog/" + slug,
    "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Li Wei" },
    "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Best Shanghai Restaurants", "url": "https://shanghai-restaurant.com" },
  })} />
</Layout>
